{"wont":{"id":"200df3f8-b5f3-4711-a422-47bb568047d1","word":"wont","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"to be accustomed","word":"soer"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"to be accustomed","word":"acostumar"}],"pos":"verb","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"I have heard it remarked by the old farmers, that when beasts are first transferred from one place to another, that if they keep them without food for two or three days, it will go far towards wonting them to their new situation.","ref":"1830, [Joseph Plumb Martin], “Campaign of 1780”, in A Narrative of Some of the Adventures, Dangers and Sufferings of a Revolutionary Soldier; Interspersed with Anecdotes of Incidents that Occurred within His Own Observation, Hallowell, Me.: Printed by Glazier, Masters & Co. No. 1, Kennebec-Row, →OCLC, page 141:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[193,200]],"id":"33c28bc1-b833-4dc6-94af-be9453e5af81"}],"categories":["English terms with archaic senses","English terms with quotations","English transitive verbs"],"links":[["accustom","accustom"]],"raw_glosses":["(transitive, archaic) To make (someone) used to; to accustom."],"glosses":["To make (someone) used to; to accustom."],"tags":["archaic","transitive"],"id":"8524f228-ab08-42d9-94a8-50c97c1cc56f"},{"examples":[{"text":"What be the ſweet delights of learning a treaſure, / That wont with Comick ſock to beautify / The painted Theaters, and fill with pleaſure / The liſtners eyes, and eares with melodie; […]","ref":"c. 1580, Edmund Spenser, “The Teares of the Mvses[: Thalia]”, in Complaints: Containing Sundrie Small Poemes of the Worlds Vanitie. VVhereof the Next Page Maketh Mention, London: Imprinted for VVilliam Ponsonbie, dwelling in Paules Churchyard at the signe of the Bishops head, published 1591, →OCLC; republished in “The Teares of the Mvses[: Thalia]”, in The Faerie Qveen: The Shepheards Calendar: Together with the Other Works of England's Arch-Pöet, Edm. Spenser: Collected into One Volume, and Carefully Corrected, London: Printed by H[umphrey] L[ownes] for Mathew Lownes, 1617, →OCLC","id":"0685c36a-b899-4248-b8eb-836c354e7973"},{"text":"But by record of antique times I finde / That wemen wont in warres to beare most ſway, / And to all great exploites them ſelues inclind: […]","ref":"1590, Edmund Spenser, The Faerie Qveene. Disposed into Twelue Books, Fashioning XII. Morall Vertues, London: Printed [by John Wolfe] for VVilliam Ponsonbie, →OCLC, book III, canto II, stanza II, page 411:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[52,56]],"id":"90d1f1e4-f5a6-41e6-877c-3b200a51b1e1"}],"categories":["English intransitive verbs","English terms with archaic senses","English terms with quotations"],"links":[["accustomed","accustomed"],["habit","habit#Noun"]],"raw_glosses":["(intransitive, archaic) To be accustomed (to something), to be in the habit (of doing something)."],"glosses":["To be accustomed (to something), to be in the habit (of doing something)."],"tags":["archaic","intransitive"],"id":"61aa0c54-8b75-427a-9911-acb99f521bd2"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"wonder":{"id":"2ccbb656-12cb-47e8-ab31-c918967bc16a","word":"wonder","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"to ponder about something","word":"ponderar"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"to ponder about something","word":"perguntar-se"}],"pos":"verb","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"He's married by now, I shouldn't wonder.","bold_text_offsets":[[33,39]],"type":"example","id":"23a7dcc6-dbfd-4e71-b431-c97270d4b6b4"},{"text":"[…]I could not ſufficiently wonder at the Intrepidity of theſe diminutive Mortals,[…]","ref":"1726 October 28, [Jonathan Swift], “The Author Gives Some Account of Himself and Family, His First Inducements to Travel. […]”, in Travels into Several Remote Nations of the World. […] [Gulliver’s Travels], volume I, London: […] Benj[amin] Motte, […], →OCLC, part I (A Voyage to Lilliput), pages 14–15:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[28,34]],"id":"254521a5-9bf2-42b4-831c-f5e7d764b8df"},{"text":"Some had read the manuſcript, and rectified its inaccuracies; others had ſeen it in a ſtate ſo imperfect, that they could not forbear to wonder at its preſent excellence; […]","ref":"1751 October 18 (Gregorian calendar), Samuel Johnson, “No. 163. Monday, October 8. 1751.”, in The Rambler, volume VII, Edinburgh: […] Sands, Murray, and Cochran; sold by W. Gordon, C. Wright, J. Yair, […], published 1752, →OCLC, page 35:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[137,143]],"id":"ff67bc75-3c52-44a7-a9ee-8acd07a6a2a4"},{"text":"The Celebrity, by arts unknown, induced Mrs. Judge Short and two other ladies to call at Mohair on a certain afternoon when Mr. Cooke was trying a trotter on the track. The three returned wondering and charmed with Mrs. Cooke; they were sure she had had no hand in the furnishing of that atrocious house.","ref":"1897 December (indicated as 1898), Winston Churchill, chapter IV, in The Celebrity: An Episode, New York, N.Y.: The Macmillan Company; London: Macmillan & Co., Ltd., →OCLC, page 58:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[188,197]],"id":"c0384956-9330-4532-a0f9-9ead24204b4d"}],"categories":["English intransitive verbs","English terms with quotations","English terms with usage examples"],"links":[["surprise","surprise"],["struck","struck"],["astonishment","astonishment"],["amazed","amazed"],["marvel","marvel"],["at","at#English"]],"raw_glosses":["(intransitive) To be affected with surprise or admiration; to be struck with astonishment; to be amazed; to marvel; often followed by at."],"glosses":["To be affected with surprise or admiration; to be struck with astonishment; to be amazed; to marvel; often followed by at."],"tags":["intransitive"],"id":"5244cea8-2216-4101-89ed-26fdbb3a6a97"},{"examples":[{"text":"He wondered whether penguins could fly. She had wondered this herself sometimes.","bold_text_offsets":[[3,11],[48,56]],"type":"example","id":"31f285fb-f878-45ff-a0b7-f1e08ef01d84"},{"text":"Has Jay gone fishing, I wonder?","bold_text_offsets":[[24,30]],"type":"example","id":"c15e2724-975e-4ba9-9b03-94ab50751992"},{"text":"I was wondering what time the film was starting.","bold_text_offsets":[[6,15]],"type":"example","id":"52cf7fae-f3a4-41db-a087-92e8766e87ae"},{"text":"I wonder in my Soule / What you would aske me, that I ſhould deny[…]","ref":"c. 1603–1604 (date written), William Shakespeare, “The Tragedie of Othello, the Moore of Venice”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act III, scene iii], page 323, column 1:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[2,8]],"id":"b3efcae8-fff2-421d-98de-1ddd72245685"},{"text":"Topping it all off, The National Alliance is an expensive, professionally-produced newspaper that is distributed for free all over the place. One wonders: Where does their money come from? Who is supporting them?","ref":"1988 December 4, John Kyper, “Avoid Friends Like The New Alliance Party”, in Gay Community News, volume 16, number 21, page 4:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[146,153]],"id":"01aea928-54ae-4d04-b1a9-eda1d5bc66b8"}],"categories":["English intransitive verbs","English terms with quotations","English terms with usage examples","English transitive verbs"],"links":[["ponder","ponder"],["doubt","doubt"],["curiosity","curiosity"]],"raw_glosses":["(transitive, intransitive) To ponder; to feel doubt and curiosity; to query in the mind."],"glosses":["To ponder; to feel doubt and curiosity; to query in the mind."],"tags":["intransitive","transitive"],"id":"5362be46-7943-4c1d-98c9-e17115798f2c"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[{"sense":"to ponder","word":"ponder"}]},"wonderful":{"id":"5bea52de-da59-4ff3-a069-1913625b9046","word":"wonderful","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"excellent, extremely impressive","word":"maravilhoso"}],"pos":"adj","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"His delusion was not wonderful, but most natural.","bold_text_offsets":[[21,30]],"type":"example","id":"19889c3b-b3d2-48bd-8640-fb3fbe10789b"},{"text":"It is not wonderful that Mrs Smith declined to visit the beer garden, in view of her militant temperance. [=It is no wonder that she declined.]","bold_text_offsets":[[10,19]],"type":"example","id":"5f11c1aa-3fa1-486b-be32-116c2a4acb77"},{"text":"'Ha!' cried Dangerfield, with a sort of gasp, and a violent smirk, the joyousness of which was, however, counteracted by a lurid scowl and a wonderful livid glare in his wild eyes; […]","ref":"1863, Sheridan Le Fanu, The House by the Churchyard:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[141,150]],"id":"b10d1669-5785-4b58-a968-8cfa997328e5"},{"text":"The difference in the circumstances of the kingdom at the two dates might fairly account for the change of policy in regard to the Solway. In 1563 the two countries were held by distinct and hostile governments, and it was not wonderful that the debateable ground of the Solway should be exempted from restrictions which could not be enforced over its whole surface, and which, if enforced on the Scotch side only, would be certainly broken through by the English marauders. In 1685, however, both kingdoms were ruled over by one sovereign; a more amicable policy had, since the accession of James I., been adopted in legislation; and it might therefore be supposed that the exception in favour of the Solway had lapsed by implication, and that the fisheries of all parts of the kingdom were thenceforward to be governed by uniform statutory regulations.","ref":"1892, Charles Stewart, edited by John Campbell Shairp, A Treatise on the Law of Scotland Relating to Rights of Fishing: Comprising the Law Affecting Sea Fishing, Salmon Fishing, Trout Fishing, Oyster and Mussel Fishing, Etc. With an Appendix of Statutes and Bye-laws, 2nd edition, T. & T. Clark, pages 343-344:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[227,236]],"id":"5b8cc4a2-0960-41c0-91d4-210498f8ab1c"},{"text":"He is massively corrupt. It is wonderful how the man's popularity survives.","ref":"1992, Hilary Mantel, A Place of Greater Safety, Harper Perennial, published 2007, page 278:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[31,40]],"id":"fe32fd68-1dc4-47ca-adfd-218088e9b358"}],"links":[["wonder","wonder"],["surprising","surprising"],["extraordinary","extraordinary"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"wondrous"},{"word":"amazing"},{"word":"astonishing"},{"word":"marvelous"},{"word":"awesome"},{"word":"amazing","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"astounding","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"awe-inspiring","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"awesome","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"awful","source":"Thesaurus:awesome","tags":["archaic"]},{"word":"sensational","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"awesomesauce","source":"Thesaurus:awesome","tags":["slang"]},{"word":"based","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"beatific","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"bewildering","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"blissful","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"celestial","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"cool","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"delightful","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"divine","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"dope","source":"Thesaurus:awesome","tags":["slang"]},{"word":"enchanting","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"excellent","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"extraordinary","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"far out","source":"Thesaurus:awesome","tags":["dated","slang"]},{"word":"fire","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"heavenlike","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"heavenly","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"hellagood","source":"Thesaurus:awesome","tags":["US","slang"]},{"word":"impressive","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"incredible","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"inspiring","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"goshwow","source":"Thesaurus:awesome","tags":["dated","slang"]},{"word":"glorious","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"gnarly","source":"Thesaurus:awesome","tags":["dated","slang"]},{"word":"goated","source":"Thesaurus:awesome","tags":["Internet"]},{"word":"grand","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"grandiose","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"great","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"groovy","source":"Thesaurus:awesome","tags":["dated","slang"]},{"word":"magnificent","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"major","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"majestic","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"marvellous","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"mystifying","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"mythic","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"neat","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"lit","source":"Thesaurus:awesome","tags":["slang"]},{"word":"outstanding","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"phenomenal","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"powerful","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"rad","source":"Thesaurus:awesome","tags":["slang"]},{"word":"righteous","source":"Thesaurus:awesome","tags":["US","slang"]},{"word":"selcouth","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"splendent","source":"Thesaurus:awesome","tags":["archaic"]},{"word":"stupendous","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"stupefying","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"sublime","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"surpassing","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"terrific","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"tight","source":"Thesaurus:awesome","tags":["slang"]},{"word":"unbelievable","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"unreal","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"wicked","source":"Thesaurus:awesome","tags":["dated","slang"]},{"word":"wonderful","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"},{"word":"wondrous","source":"Thesaurus:awesome"}],"antonyms":[{"word":"unsurprising"},{"word":"unremarkable"},{"word":"common"}],"categories":["English terms with quotations","English terms with usage examples"],"glosses":["Tending to excite wonder; surprising, extraordinary."],"id":"6cc72ccc-d8af-4c31-a3a9-a233cf5c2982"},{"examples":[{"text":"They served a wonderful six-course meal.","bold_text_offsets":[[14,23]],"type":"example","id":"47ae1d2d-9582-4240-8615-30778a26e181"},{"text":"What appears to be wonderful may turn out to be anything but.","bold_text_offsets":[[19,28]],"type":"example","id":"267b1236-d7c6-47e6-85b7-d971fb6b90c3"},{"text":"Though they obviously realized that these episodes were part of something wonderful and important and lasting, the writers and producers couldn’t have imagined that 20 years later “Treehouse Of Horror” wouldn’t just survive; it’d thrive as one of the most talked-about and watched episodes of every season of The Simpsons.","ref":"2012 April 29, Nathan Rabin, “TV: Review: THE SIMPSONS (CLASSIC): “Treehouse of Horror III” (season 4, episode 5; originally aired 10/29/1992)”, in (Please provide the book title or journal name):","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[74,83]],"id":"81242452-a706-4039-882f-feec36f02ade"}],"links":[["excellent","excellent"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"great"},{"word":"amazing"},{"word":"marvelous"},{"word":"fantastic"},{"word":"awesome"},{"word":"terrific"},{"word":"ace","source":"Thesaurus:excellent","tags":["Britain","slang"]},{"word":"admirable","source":"Thesaurus:excellent"},{"word":"amazing","source":"Thesaurus:excellent"},{"word":"awe-inspiring","source":"Thesaurus:excellent"},{"word":"awesome","source":"Thesaurus:excellent","tags":["colloquial"]},{"word":"badass","source":"Thesaurus:excellent"},{"word":"bang on","source":"Thesaurus:excellent"},{"word":"bang-up","source":"Thesaurus:excellent"},{"word":"studly","source":"Thesaurus:excellent"},{"word":"based","source":"Thesaurus:excellent"},{"word":"beatific","source":"Thesaurus:excellent"},{"word":"beneship","source":"Thesaurus:excellent"},{"word":"bitching","source":"Thesaurus:excellent","tags":["slang"]},{"word":"b